{\pard\keepn\sb800\sa180\fs34\b\cf1 Incense at Maroma is still made by hand\par}
{\pard\sl400\slmult1\sb40\sa220\fs26\b Maroma still makes incense by hand in Auroville, using woods, resins, herbs, and flowers in a method the company traces to older Indian practice, when those same materials were burned to cleanse a room, support prayer, and quiet the mind.\par}
{\pard\sl380\slmult1\sb80\sa180 Maroma says it keeps that sequence on the bench: select, blend, shape, and dry each stick.\par}
{\pard\sl380\slmult1\sb80\sa180 The company said the ingredients, the people who form them, and the time the process takes remain central to the work. Smoke, in this account, is treated as part of a room ritual, not only as a scent.\par}
{\pard\sl380\slmult1\sb80\sa180 The incense range is sold as charcoal-free and made without phthalates. It is listed on maroma.com.\par}
